From ec7ffbd1e0ca30760832fe67e2b724e737adcc90 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VERHILLE Arnaud <gist974@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 14:58:33 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 20/20] 68020/68030: report a locked read-modify-write fault with RM

MC68020UM 6.2.1 and MC68030UM 8.2.1 define the RM bit of the special status
word: "Read-modify-write on data cycle". The core never set it. A bus error
in a data cycle of TAS, CAS or CAS2 now sets RM, in the read cycle and in the
write cycle alike; the cycles that compute the effective address and the
instruction fetches are not locked and keep RM clear. Frames supplied by the
host are left untouched; the 68040 (LK) is not covered.

MC68020UM 6.2.2 and MC68030UM 8.2.2: a handler emulates the entire locked
instruction, clears DF and leaves RM set, "because the RTE instruction
expects the entire operation to have been completed if the RM bit is set
and the DF bit is cleared. This is true even if the fault occurred on the
first read cycle." RTE now continues behind the instruction in that case.
Its end is captured when the fault occurs (all instruction words have been
read by then) and kept in an internal register of the bus fault frame
(offset $1C). With DF set, RTE reruns the entire instruction as before
(MC68020UM 6.2.3, MC68030UM 8.2.3).

Before, such a handler looped: RTE restarted the instruction, which faulted
again (findings_repro case 6: 14 bus errors where the manual expects one).
